WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:05.820
- [Announcer] Garlands,
gift bags, and applause.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.430
This plane full of Chinese
tourists was greeted in Thailand

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:10.770
by cabinet ministers
and a crowd of onlookers

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.270
holding welcoming banners.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:17.130
- Now we can say Thailand
freely opened for all tourists.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:18.450
- [Announcer] They were the
first to visit the country

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.040
since China reopened its borders

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.590
after three years of heavy restrictions.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:23.610
Before the pandemic,

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.380
Chinese travelers were the largest source

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.970
of tourism revenue globally,

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:30.243
spending $255 billion around the world.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.300
But travel analysts say limited flights,

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.730
travel restrictions, and lower demand

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.680
are slowing down the return
of these big spending,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.933
international visitors, at least for now.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.540
For nearly three years,
strict quarantine requirements

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.310
have kept millions of Chinese tourists

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:48.600
from traveling abroad.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.760
Travel into and out of mainland China

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.900
dropped by more than 94% to
8.8 million trips in 2021,

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.780
from 170 million in 2019.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.630
But in January, China's government
removed the restrictions

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.520
that stifled the flow of these travelers,

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.620
who were particularly
important for tourism hotspots

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.770
in the US, Europe, and Southeast Asia.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.210
Take Singapore for example,

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.650
where visitors from mainland China

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:14.040
made up nearly a fifth of
international arrivals in 2019

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.800
and spent around $3.1 billion.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.420
Businesses in Southeast Asia have lost

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.600
an estimated 6 million
tourism jobs since 2019

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.750
and have struggled to find
alternative sources of revenue.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.910
Now, even as some countries
begin welcoming back

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.550
Chinese tourists, analysts
say the regional industry

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.550
won't recover until 2024,
and maybe even longer

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.110
for the rest of the world.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.063
So why will recovery take so long?

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.720
Firstly, flight capacity is limited.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.880
When China closed its borders
to international travel

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.700
in 2020, many airlines
rerouted their fleets

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.470
to serve other destinations.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:48.990
And now, with high fuel
and operational costs,

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.630
many are not rushing to
resume pre-pandemic routes

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:52.950
to and from China.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.140
Days after China reopened its borders,

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.140
there were only 16 international
flights scheduled to depart

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.423
from Beijing Capital
International Airport.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.020
And then there's the travel restrictions.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.450
Some governments are wary
about rising COVID cases

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.360
coming out of China and have
implemented travel rules.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.260
In December, Italy,

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:11.970
traditionally among the top destinations

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.610
for Chinese tourists
became the first EU country

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.410
to introduce COVID testing on arrival

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.760
for those coming from China.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.040
Many other countries have
announced similar requirements,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.440
prompting China to criticize the measures.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.190
(speaks in foreign language)

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.253
- [Announcer] And lastly,

00:02:31.253 --> 00:02:33.540
the demand for traveling
abroad is still low.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.940
Today, flights in and
out of Northeast Asia,

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.680
the region that includes China,

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.800
are still about 60% below
pre-pandemic levels.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.230
And the lunar new year holidays
are expected to see people

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:46.410
mostly travel domestically
as they reunite with family.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.870
That means demand for
international flights won't pick up

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.963
until the second quarter of the
year, according to analysts.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.980
The ripple effects of this
delayed recovery in travel

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.320
have wide economic implications.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.090
Sectors that are connected to tourism,

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:02.160
like hospitality and luxury goods,

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.590
have suffered while Chinese
tourists stayed home.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:07.560
Shares of some luxury goods
companies such as LVMH

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.120
have rallied since China announced

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.530
the easing of restrictions.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.630
And analysts say it's
just one of the industries

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.850
that have high hopes for
when Chinese travelers

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:15.903
start spending again.

